homtom: show oops and panic on the LCD, keep a RAM last-log

With the GUI running, the LCD console is muted (/sys/kernel/homtom_splash
or homtom.quiet), and an oops that did reach the screen was drawn over the
GUI pixels, with the first lines ("Unable to handle kernel ...") missing.

homtom-fbtext.c:
 - The first console write with oops_in_progress while muted clears the
   screen and starts at the top, and turns the panel on (TPAL off,
   backlight, ENVID).
 - A kmsg dumper (KMSG_DUMP_OOPS and KMSG_DUMP_PANIC) copies the newest
   16 KiB of the log into a static buffer, finds the first line of the
   latest incident ("Unable to handle kernel", "Internal error:",
   "kernel BUG at", "BUG:", chained back over up to 4 lines; else the
   "Kernel panic" line; an oops head more than 150 lines before the panic
   line is treated as an older incident), redraws the screen from there
   with timestamps stripped, pins the "Kernel panic" line to the bottom
   row, and freezes the console so the panic tail cannot scroll it away.
   No allocation, no locks.

Board file:
 - homtom_panic_lcd_on(): GPB0 output high (backlight full, overrides the
   PWM that homtom-bl set), LCDCON1 ENVID on. Raw register writes.
 - pstore/ramoops at 0x33E00000, 64 KiB (memblock_reserve'd): two 16 KiB
   oops/panic records and a 16 KiB console ring, uncached, no ECC, no
   compression. After the next boot: mount -t pstore pstore /sys/fs/pstore.
   Whether TTBL leaves this RAM alone across a reset is unverified.

Defconfig: CONFIG_PANIC_TIMEOUT=10 (panic restarts after 10 s through the
0004 path, INFORM0=5; before this the panic hung until the watchdog
fired), CONFIG_PSTORE=y, CONFIG_PSTORE_RAM=y, CONFIG_PSTORE_CONSOLE=y,
CONFIG_PSTORE_DEFLATE_COMPRESS off.

Applies with -p1 after 0005.

